<Project>

  <!--
    FS.GG.Kit consumer defaults (ADR-0062). Auto-imported by NuGet from the package's build/ folder.
    Every knob is overridable from the consumer BEFORE this import (a PackageReference imports build/
    props early), or from a Directory.Build.props.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- The repo root the kit materializes into. Skills land under <root>/<skill-root>/<name>/SKILL.md,
         the client at <root>/scripts/fsgg-coord, the engine manifest at <root>/.config/dotnet-tools.json.
         Defaults to the referencing project's directory; a receiver whose kit lives at the repo root sets
         this to that root (e.g. in a repo-root Directory.Build.props: $(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)). -->
    <FsggKitReceiverRoot Condition="'$(FsggKitReceiverRoot)' == ''">$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)</FsggKitReceiverRoot>

    <!-- The skill roots this package MATERIALIZES INTO, ';'-separated. ADR-0065 makes this the same
         universal root contract used by fsgg-sdd and coordination-sync — TWO roots since ADR-0067 §5
         (.github#1636) retired `.codex/skills`, which is Codex's other native root and produced only
         a duplicate catalog entry. Receivers drop it by RE-MATERIALIZING, never by hand.

         NARROWED TO ONE — ADR-0067 §9 STAGE 2 (.github#1676), 2026-07-29. This default used to read
         `.claude/skills;.agents/skills`: the package materialized real bytes into BOTH. It no longer
         does, because `.agents/skills` is now a generated VIEW root (see FsggKitViewSkillRoots below)
         whose content is produced at checkout and never transported.

         THE RUNTIME CONTRACT IS UNCHANGED, and that is what makes this stage 2 rather than a
         retirement: the runtime surface is the UNION of this property and FsggKitViewSkillRoots, and
         that union is still ADR-0065's two. A root moving from the first property to the second keeps
         the union constant.

         IT CHANGES NO RECEIVER'S ANSWER TODAY — measured across all seven on 2026-07-28: every one
         already OVERRIDES both properties inline with exactly these values, which is stage 1 (7 of 7,
         complete). What it fixes is the receiver that does NOT override: before, it materialized real
         bytes into a root that should be generated — the defect FS.GG.SDD#770 and
         FS.GG.Governance#338 each closed one tool at a time. -->
    <FsggKitSkillRoots Condition="'$(FsggKitSkillRoots)' == ''">.claude/skills</FsggKitSkillRoots>

    <!-- Roots the contract has RETIRED, ';'-separated. The materializer removes the kit's own skill
         directories from each on the receiver's next restore, so a retirement completes itself
         instead of leaving every receiver an orphaned tree to delete by hand. Retiring a root without
         listing it here is a no-op on receivers: the materializer only walks FsggKitSkillRoots. -->
    <FsggKitRetiredSkillRoots Condition="'$(FsggKitRetiredSkillRoots)' == ''">.codex/skills</FsggKitRetiredSkillRoots>

    <!-- VIEW ROOTS — the THIRD disposition, ';'-separated (ADR-0065 §Retiring a root, .github#1696).
         Empty by default, and that default is the whole safety of this property: it changes NOTHING
         for any receiver until that receiver opts in, so shipping the mechanism retires nothing
         (ADR-0067 §9, which owns the retirement).

         A view root is a root that is STILL IN THE RUNTIME CONTRACT — both runtimes must find skills
         there, and `Fsgg.Schemas.agentSkillRoots` / `.agent-skill-roots` / `coordination-sync` /
         `KitDigest` still count it — but whose CONTENT is generated locally at checkout by
         `scripts/skill-view generate` rather than materialized into by this package. It is exactly
         what `FsggKitSkillRoots` and `FsggKitRetiredSkillRoots` between them could not express, and
         the gap is not cosmetic: without it a receiver's second root stays COMMITTED, and
         `scripts/skill-view:331` then refuses to generate over a root git tracks, with no override.
         The receiver was wedged between three correct rules.

         Declaring it RETIRED instead would be wrong twice: the root would drop out of the runtime
         contract while both runtimes still read it, and the root-set declarations above would then
         disagree with what the runtimes actually load.

         THE RUNTIME ROOT SET IS THE UNION. `FsggKitSkillRoots` is now the set this package
         MATERIALIZES INTO, not the runtime surface; the runtime surface is
         `FsggKitSkillRoots` + `FsggKitViewSkillRoots`, and THAT union is what must equal
         `.agent-skill-roots` / `agentSkillRoots`. A receiver that moves a root from the first
         property to this one keeps the union constant, which is why the move is not a root retirement.

         WHAT THE MATERIALIZER DOES WITH ONE (build/FS.GG.Kit.targets):
           1. it does NOT copy skills into it — the view is generated, not transported;
           2. it REMOVES the kit's own skill directories an older package materialized there, exactly
              as it does for a retired root, so the receiver never hand-deletes a mirror (ADR-0065's
              transport contract) and `skill-view generate` is no longer refused for a tracked root;
           3. it then ASSERTS every kit skill is visible there and FAILS LOUD when it is not — the
              §8 half. A root nothing materializes into is a root nothing verifies, and ADR-0067 §8
              is explicit that "a rewrite that removes the loud failure and adds the quiet one is
              worse than no rewrite." That assertion is also invocable on its own, with no
              materialize, as `-t:FsggKitCheckSkillView`.

         A root may not appear in more than one of the three properties; the materializer refuses,
         rather than silently picking a disposition.

         DEFAULTED TO `.agents/skills` — ADR-0067 §9 STAGE 2 (.github#1676), 2026-07-29. The paragraph
         above still describes the EMPTY default as "the whole safety of this property". That was
         exactly right while receivers were opting in ONE AT A TIME, and §9's precondition for stage 2
         is that they all have. THEY HAVE: stage 1 is 7 of 7, and every receiver declares this property
         inline with this value, so flipping the default changes no receiver's evaluated answer. It
         changes what a receiver gets when it does not say — which is now the end state rather than the
         state §9 was retiring. -->
    <FsggKitViewSkillRoots Condition="'$(FsggKitViewSkillRoots)' == ''">.agents/skills</FsggKitViewSkillRoots>

    <!-- Materialize automatically as part of the consumer's build. A receiver that would rather
         materialize on an explicit `dotnet build -t:FsggKitMaterialize` sets this false. -->
    <FsggKitMaterializeOnBuild Condition="'$(FsggKitMaterializeOnBuild)' == ''">true</FsggKitMaterializeOnBuild>

    <!-- Also materialize the build-config capability (dist/dotnet Directory.Build.props +
         Directory.Packages.props) to <root>/. OPT-IN and OFF by default: build-config reaches only 4
         receivers (sdd/rendering/governance/game), not templates/audio/net, and .github imports rather
         than copies it (.github#609). A receiver that today `receives: build-config` sets this true; the
         materialized files are committed, exactly as the sync-build-config byte-copies are, so this is
         the write arm that replaces that copy — not a live per-build input. global.json stays UNMANAGED
         (.github#903) and is not carried. -->
    <FsggKitMaterializeBuildConfig Condition="'$(FsggKitMaterializeBuildConfig)' == ''">false</FsggKitMaterializeBuildConfig>

    <!-- First-time build-config adoption (.github#387 safety). OFF by default: the materialize REFUSES
         to overwrite a pre-existing, hand-authored .props (one lacking the 'Source of truth: FS-GG/.github'
         marker) rather than silently clobber it. A build-config receiver adopting the package sets this
         true for ONE run — it moves the hand-authored Directory.Build.props / Directory.Packages.props to
         Directory.Build.local.props / Directory.Packages.local.props (which the canonical file imports, so
         repo-specific settings survive), then writes the canonical marked file. After that the .props
         carries the marker and materialize overwrites it freely, so this can go back to false. This is the
         package-side equivalent of sync-build-config.sh's adopt mode. -->
    <FsggKitAdoptBuildConfig Condition="'$(FsggKitAdoptBuildConfig)' == ''">false</FsggKitAdoptBuildConfig>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
